otal": 6.35, "baseline_margin": 6.1, "analyst_adjustment": 0.9, "key_factors": ["Prediction markets price Bennett near 91-92% to win", "GOP super PAC pulled $10M from Kean's defense, signaling triage", "All ten expert ratings call the seat a Democratic flip, mostly Lean D", "September poll shows Bennett up 5 among likely voters"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Democratic-leaning environment in an even-PVI district, and the September poll plus a broad Lean/Likely D rater consensus support Bennett. Markets price her near 91%, and the GOP super PAC's $10M withdrawal suggests Republicans see the seat as slipping, justifying a Likely D call.", "watch": "A late Republican recommitment of outside money or a polling shift back toward Kean could move this back toward Lean D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Thomas Kean Jr. (R) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term Republican Tom Kean Jr. is defending an even-PVI New Jersey district in what polls and headlines describe as a favorable Democratic environment. Challenger Rebecca Bennett, a former Navy pilot, has kept pace financially, raising $4.3 million to Kean's $4.9 million, and outside groups have spent more to support her than to back the incumbent. A September likely-voter poll showed Bennett ahead by five points.\n\nEvery major race rater now lists the seat as a Democratic flip, ranging from Tilt D to Likely D, and prediction markets put Bennett's chances above ninety percent. The most telling signal is Republican behavior: a GOP super PAC pulled roughly $10 million out of New Jersey House races including this one, suggesting the party is triaging once-competitive seats. Kean retains a large cash advantage and some local endorsements, but the weight of evidence favors a Democratic pickup.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8072, "margin": 5.29}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8017, "margin": 5.2}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8142, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p